From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: ppe: v1: offload L2 bridge flows as L3 subflows

The FoE v1 backend (EN751221) only accepted TCP/UDP 5-tuple flows, so the
flower rules installed by bridger for bridged traffic were rejected and a
software bridge between the GPON port and a LAN port topped out at ~330
Mbit/s with the CPU saturated.

The PPE hashes bridged IP traffic by its L3/L4 tuple, like routed traffic,
so follow the mainline approach: keep an L2 flow (exact MAC pair, optional
ingress VLAN, redirect and VLAN push/pop) as an output template only, and
when a packet of a matching MAC pair hits an unbound bucket, create an L3
subflow for its tuple with unchanged addresses and MACs, the PPPoE session
of the packet and the output of the L2 flow, then bind it like any other v1
flow. Subflows idle for 30 s are reaped when an L2 flow reaches 256 of
them; destroying the L2 flow frees them all, and its statistics report the
most recent subflow activity so bridger keeps it. debugfs ppe/regs gains
l2_subflows, l2_subflow_full, l2_lookups and l2_misses.

Measured on an XR500v bridging PPPoE between GPON and a LAN port: 680
Mbit/s down at 9-14% CPU, against 124-330 Mbit/s at 100% in software.

